WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.570
Sports day results came in a heap of

2
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.070
a hundred times, in no order.

3
00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:08.610
Two students sorted them in

4
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.750
two different ways.

5
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.880
Both got it right, but one

6
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.580
did ten times the work.

7
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.040
Today: sorting, and how to

8
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:18.980
judge an algorithm.

9
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:21.760
Bubble sort is the simplest.

10
00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.250
Compare each pair of neighbours, and swap

11
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:26.690
them if they’re in the wrong order.

12
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.080
After one pass, the biggest

13
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.740
value has bubbled to the end.

14
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.050
Repeat until a pass makes no swaps.

15
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.010
Insertion sort works like

16
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:38.590
sorting cards in your hand.

17
00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:41.870
Take the next card, and slide it left

18
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.530
past every bigger one until it fits.

19
00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.310
On a list that’s nearly sorted, almost

20
00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.150
nothing has to move, so it’s very fast.

21
00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.270
Merge sort is cleverer.

22
00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.550
Split the list in half, sort each half,

23
00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.690
then merge the two sorted halves by taking

24
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.530
the smaller front item each time.

25
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.780
For big lists it does far less work

26
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.660
than bubble or insertion sort.

27
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.670
To compare algorithms fairly, count their

28
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.950
steps, not the seconds on one phone.

29
00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.810
Bubble sort on n items does about n

30
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.310
times n, divided by two, comparisons.

31
00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:23.390
Merge sort does about n times log n.

32
00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:27.030
For a thousand items, that’s about half a

33
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.210
million against about ten thousand.

34
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:30.850
Quick check!

35
00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.810
A list of marks is already sorted,

36
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.970
except two neighbours swapped.

37
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.940
Which sort finishes with the fewest steps?

38
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.250
Insertion sort.

39
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.960
Merge sort is best in general, but the

40
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.500
best choice depends on the data.

41
00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.020
On a nearly sorted list, insertion

42
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.540
sort barely moves anything.

43
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.080
But speed means nothing if

44
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.100
the answer is wrong.

45
00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.590
Judge an algorithm by correctness first.

46
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.630
Test it on the edge cases: an empty list,

47
00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.850
one item, a list already sorted, a list in

48
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.930
reverse, and repeated values.

49
00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.190
You can even let the computer check.

50
00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.680
A function, is sorted, walks through the

51
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.380
list and compares each item with the next.

52
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.660
If any pair is in the wrong

53
00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.760
order, the list isn’t sorted.

54
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.280
Run it after every sort you write.

55
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.810
In the app, open Sorting race.

56
00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:34.730
Race eight bars, then thirty-two: bubble

57
00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.230
sort needs about sixteen times the

58
00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.070
comparisons, merge sort only about six.

59
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:43.260
Then choose nearly sorted and

60
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.780
watch insertion sort win.

61
00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:46.760
Your turn.

62
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.980
Your sort passes on five random lists.

63
00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.480
Which test should you add first?

64
00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.680
The edge cases.

65
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.940
Remember: bubble sort swaps

66
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.160
neighbours; insertion slides each item

67
00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.520
into place; merge splits and merges.

68
00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.750
Judge correctness first,

69
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.430
then count the work.

70
00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.270
And the best choice depends on the data.

71
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.280
Try it yourself: write ten numbers on

72
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.540
cards and sort them twice, once by bubble

73
00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.140
sort and once by insertion sort.

74
00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.250
Count every comparison.

75
00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.120
Which needed fewer, and why?
